      "heading": "2. Phylum Mollusca & Echinodermata",
      "paragraphs": [
        "**Phylum Mollusca Features:** Second largest animal phylum. Terrestrial or aquatic animals with an unsegmented, soft body covered by a calcareous shell (in most). Body is divided into distinct **Head**, **Muscular Foot** (for locomotion), and **Visceral Hump** (visceral mass). A soft, spongy layer of skin forms a **Mantle** over the visceral hump. The space between the hump and mantle is the **Mantle Cavity**, which houses feather-like gills (**ctenidia**) functioning in both respiration and excretion. The mouth contains a file-like rasping organ called **Radula** for feeding (absent in bivalves). Examples: *Pila* (Apple snail), *Pinctada* (Pearl oyster), *Sepia* (Cuttlefish), *Loligo* (Squid), *Octopus* (Devil fish), *Aplysia* (Sea hare), *Dentalium* (Tusk shell), *Chaetopleura* (Chiton).",
        "**Phylum Echinodermata Features:** Spiny-skinned marine animals with an endoskeleton of calcareous ossicles. **CRITICAL SYMMETRY RULE:** **Adults display Pentamerous Radial Symmetry, whereas free-swimming larvae display Bilateral Symmetry!** Digestion is complete with mouth on lower/ventral side and anus on upper/dorsal side. **Water Vascular System:** A unique hydraulic system consisting of a madreporite, ring canal, radial canals, and tube feet (**podia**) that functions in locomotion, capture and transport of food, and gas exchange. **Excretory system is completely ABSENT**. Examples: *Asterias* (Starfish), *Echinus* (Sea urchin), *Antedon* (Sea lily), *Cucumaria* (Sea cucumber), *Ophiura* (Brittle star)."
      ],
      "subpoints": [
        "Mollusca Structure: Soft body divided into Head, Muscular Foot, and Visceral Mass. Mantle covers visceral mass; mantle cavity contains feather-like gills (ctenidia) for respiration and excretion. Mouth contains Radula.",
        "Echinodermata Water Vascular System: Hydraulic system with tube feet (podia) assisting locomotion, capture and transport of food, and respiration. Adults have 5-part radial symmetry; larvae have bilateral symmetry."
      ]
